WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:17.150
MARIA BARTIROMO: Good morning, everyone.

00:00:17.150 --> 00:00:18.410
Great to see you this morning.

00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:23.220
Thank you for coming out and joining us today
for the CFR meeting.

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:24.550
Welcome to all of you.

00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:25.550
Welcome to our members.

00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:28.609
This, of course, part of our CEO Speaker Series.

00:00:28.609 --> 00:00:33.030
And we've got a really exciting and interesting
morning for you.

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:34.470
Just a couple of notes.

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:41.090
Please turn off all your cell phones -- don't
put it toward "vibrate," because it does impact

00:00:41.090 --> 00:00:46.300
the actual transmission -- BlackBerrys, all
of the wireless devices, to avoid any interference

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:47.300
this morning.

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:48.870
We appreciate that.

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:53.010
I'm thrilled to be here with Larry Fink, who,
of course, is one of the great entrepreneurs

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:57.510
and businessmen, financial titans, of our
time.

00:00:57.510 --> 00:01:01.950
Larry started BlackRock first when it was
a part of Blackstone.

00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:09.410
He then moved -- spun it off into its own
firm and has managed to grow the firm really

00:01:09.410 --> 00:01:16.400
extraordinarily, now boasting assets of more
than $3 trillion, the largest asset manager.

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:20.910
So we really (will glean ?) great value from
our discussion with Larry this morning.

00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:22.520
So we will have a Q-and-A session.

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:24.610
We'll open it up to all of you.

00:01:24.610 --> 00:01:28.240
Of course we have terrific brilliance in the
audience, and we want to hear what's on your

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:31.540
mind, and Larry's excited to take your questions.

00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:36.729
But we'll begin with Larry talking to us about
what's going on in the world today.

00:01:36.729 --> 00:01:39.320
He of course is the chairman and CEO of Black
Rock.

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:42.100
He leads the company's global executive committee.

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:46.540
He led the firm through its entire two decades,
founded it -- co-founded it.

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:51.900
He was named CEO of the decade by Financial
News in 2011 and has been named one of the

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:56.740
world's best CEOs by Barron's each year -- each
year -- since 2005.

00:01:56.740 --> 00:02:01.551
By the way, he is also a member of the board
of trustees of New York University, chair

00:02:01.551 --> 00:02:05.690
of the financial affairs committee and a member
of the executive committee, the ad hoc committee

00:02:05.690 --> 00:02:08.450
on board governance and committee on trustees.

00:02:08.450 --> 00:02:13.019
He is traveling extensively around the world
and has access to the great minds and deep-pocketed

00:02:13.019 --> 00:02:17.250
investors globally -- and, again, all the
more reason we want to hear from him this

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:18.250
morning.

00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:20.439
So let me introduce Larry to give us his opening
remarks

00:02:20.439 --> 00:02:24.769
Ladies and gentlemen, Larry Fink.

00:02:24.769 --> 00:02:26.079
(Applause.)

00:02:26.079 --> 00:02:32.629
&gt;&gt;LAURENCE D. FINK: Welcome, everyone.

00:02:32.629 --> 00:02:36.969
Thank you, Maria, and it's really nice to
be here, especially with one of my mentors

00:02:36.969 --> 00:02:41.019
in my career, and that's Pete Peterson, who
has been so influential in the council.

00:02:41.019 --> 00:02:45.389
Pete, thank you for all the help.

00:02:45.389 --> 00:02:51.650
And much of my success is due to the -- those
few inspirational years I had with Pete at

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:52.760
Black Stone (sic).

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:55.380
I would like to welcome everyone today.

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:56.389
I know it's morning.

00:02:56.389 --> 00:02:58.870
We're going to have inclement weather.

00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:05.370
So it's -- it's always nice to see such a
hardy audience early in the morning.

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:09.639
So let me talk about my vision of the world
and where I see it.

00:03:09.639 --> 00:03:16.680
Let us -- (I'd ?) like to just -- also just
talk about -- it's really -- (audio break

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:22.569
?) -- to be a part of this session, with all
the distinguished guests here today at such

00:03:22.569 --> 00:03:25.420
a great, respected forum.

00:03:25.420 --> 00:03:30.439
And it couldn't be more important today because
-- and relevant today given how interconnected

00:03:30.439 --> 00:03:35.480
the world is and how we are so connected globally.

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:40.769
As daily headlines on European debt makes
abundantly clear, the economic destinies of

00:03:40.769 --> 00:03:44.029
people across the globe are inextricably linked.

00:03:44.029 --> 00:03:50.819
It is no wonder that for all the record, recent,
hopeful signs of economic recovery and rebounding

00:03:50.819 --> 00:03:56.609
markets, there's still a gnawing sense out
there that things are just not right.

00:03:56.609 --> 00:04:02.030
Every CEO I talk to -- and I talk to quite
a few worldwide -- are telling me virtually

00:04:02.030 --> 00:04:06.449
the same thing: that business is actually
a little better than they planned.

00:04:06.449 --> 00:04:10.419
It's -- it actually is starting move to dial.

00:04:10.419 --> 00:04:16.010
But they then cautionly say, but things just
don't feel right; they feel, in some respects,

00:04:16.010 --> 00:04:17.760
really bad.

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:23.460
So lacking this confidence in the future,
they're continuing to build their cash even

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:26.759
as earnings improve.

00:04:26.759 --> 00:04:32.220
And from kitchen tables to trading floors
to street protests in Athens, London or Manhattan,

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:36.250
people are angry, they're frustrated, they're
confused.

00:04:36.250 --> 00:04:42.190
They're worried about stagnant incomes and,
of course, eroding savings.

00:04:42.190 --> 00:04:45.980
They're worried that government promises of
secured retirement are simply impossible to

00:04:45.980 --> 00:04:51.310
meet, and they're looking for leadership,
and they're looking for answers.

00:04:51.310 --> 00:04:57.430
Fundamentally a broad crisis of confidence
is paralyzing our ability to make long-term

00:04:57.430 --> 00:05:00.310
decisions.

00:05:00.310 --> 00:05:04.919
Much of the problem is our inability to look
beyond the latest headlines as they change

00:05:04.919 --> 00:05:09.810
minute by minute, with every blog or every
website.

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:16.229
Whether it's about the Greek debt or whether
it's about gasoline prices, in today's wired

00:05:16.229 --> 00:05:20.150
world people are bombarded with so much information
and news.

00:05:20.150 --> 00:05:26.830
Even if much of the news is good, it's very
hard to interpret the good from the bad.

00:05:26.830 --> 00:05:32.129
It's affecting the business decisions from
everyone from the corner office to the corner

00:05:32.129 --> 00:05:39.639
drugstore and having a deeply unsettling effect
on the markets with dramatic swings in trading.

00:05:39.639 --> 00:05:44.880
It's this shortening time horizon, not just
for investors, but politicians and businessmen

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:51.280
-- this fixation on the short term is also
blinding society to powerful forces to -- in

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:53.639
reshaping the world.

00:05:53.639 --> 00:05:59.069
This new world, if you will, is being defined
by three far-reaching and closely intertwined

00:05:59.069 --> 00:06:06.370
trends that are disrupting markets, economies
and many sectors of society.

00:06:06.370 --> 00:06:11.319
The most -- the most sweeping, yet most overlooked
is the great aging.

00:06:11.319 --> 00:06:18.719
According to the United Nations, the over-60
age group will roughly triple in size to 2

00:06:18.719 --> 00:06:23.870
billion people in the next four years, growing
more than twice the rate of the rest of the

00:06:23.870 --> 00:06:26.690
population.

00:06:26.690 --> 00:06:31.970
This worldwide phenomenon is already placing
major strains in the entire global system.

00:06:31.970 --> 00:06:37.760
Living longer should be a blessing, but many
older people are now afraid of outliving their

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:39.060
savings.

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:46.740
In the United States alone, the combined underfunding
of Social Security and Medicare over the next

00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:54.949
75 years exceeds a shortfall of $40 trillion,
according to the program actuaries.

00:06:54.949 --> 00:07:01.030
U.S. pension funds at year end were underfunded
by an average of 33 percent, according to

00:07:01.030 --> 00:07:02.450
BlackRock.

00:07:02.450 --> 00:07:07.349
Also according to BlackRock, the average defined
contribution plan is underfunded by over 40

00:07:07.349 --> 00:07:11.389
percent, so even worse than defined benefit
plan.

00:07:11.389 --> 00:07:15.800
And around the world, we're facing an unprecedented
simultaneous withdrawal of both financial

00:07:15.800 --> 00:07:18.639
and human capital from the system.

00:07:18.639 --> 00:07:20.889
Let's just look at Japan.

00:07:20.889 --> 00:07:25.800
It's long had one of the world's highest savings
rates, which helped fund our government and

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:29.350
many other governments' deficits around the
globe.

00:07:29.350 --> 00:07:32.969
But it also has one of the world's oldest
populations.

00:07:32.969 --> 00:07:40.420
But it also has one of the -- so Japan will
soon shift from a net saver to a net seller

00:07:40.420 --> 00:07:41.759
of assets.

00:07:41.759 --> 00:07:48.900
This will have a profound impact on the -- on
the world and on government deficits.

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:54.030
Unfortunately, this great aging coincides
with a great deleveraging.

00:07:54.030 --> 00:07:58.789
The financial and corporate sector and households
across the developed world continue to reduce

00:07:58.789 --> 00:08:02.909
debt and derisk in the wake of the financial
crisis.

00:08:02.909 --> 00:08:08.729
It will shape investment, returns and spending
for years to come.

00:08:08.729 --> 00:08:13.169
As The New York Times pointed out last week,
anxiety about assuming long-term obligations

00:08:13.169 --> 00:08:19.301
is shifting in the United States from a nation
of homeowners to a nation of renters, even

00:08:19.301 --> 00:08:29.669
as we witness rising rent prices and affordability
for housing at -- almost at a record level.

00:08:29.669 --> 00:08:33.640
And the global deleveraging is being compounded
as governments around the world pursue austerity

00:08:33.640 --> 00:08:36.090
plans to address deficit spending.

00:08:36.090 --> 00:08:40.100
Governments won't be a source of job growth
going forward, as it has been in -- for many

00:08:40.100 --> 00:08:42.270
years in the United States.

00:08:42.270 --> 00:08:50.540
So job creation will require a much more meaningful
partnership between government and business.

00:08:50.540 --> 00:08:54.770
And further complicating these challenges
is the great migration of the engines of global

00:08:54.770 --> 00:08:57.910
growth to the emerging markets.

00:08:57.910 --> 00:09:03.411
Over time, this shift of economic opportunity
will help create a more stable world, but

00:09:03.411 --> 00:09:07.600
for now it's fueling much more global anxiety.

00:09:07.600 --> 00:09:12.850
It's driving global disparity in income on
both ends of that migration.

00:09:12.850 --> 00:09:18.450
Those adept at navigating a global economy
have adopted and even benefited while many

00:09:18.450 --> 00:09:22.290
others have been left behind.

00:09:22.290 --> 00:09:26.370
So we shouldn't be surprised by the social
unrest it caused, whether it's Occupy Wall

00:09:26.370 --> 00:09:32.200
Street or the Arab Spring, with its powerful
economic undercurrents.

00:09:32.200 --> 00:09:38.020
To sum it all up, we're in a perfect storm
right now: aging population, deleveraging

00:09:38.020 --> 00:09:43.960
economies, shifting jobs, income inequality
wrapped up in low growth and low yields -- just

00:09:43.960 --> 00:09:51.420
when people need better returns and a better
economic opportunity.

00:09:51.420 --> 00:09:55.220
So in the midst of the perfect storm, with
everyone focused on the next change of the

00:09:55.220 --> 00:10:00.820
prevailing winds, with confidence so scarce
and volatility so broad, I get asked the same

00:10:00.820 --> 00:10:03.690
question wherever I am.

00:10:03.690 --> 00:10:05.620
I could be in Abu Dhabi.

00:10:05.620 --> 00:10:10.470
I could be in Beijing, Sao Paulo, Zurich or
right here in Manhattan.

00:10:10.470 --> 00:10:17.090
The question is simple: What do I do with
my money?

00:10:17.090 --> 00:10:23.190
Whether it comes from an individual, a corporation
or a pension fund, my answer is all the same:

00:10:23.190 --> 00:10:26.140
You need to get off the sidelines and get
your money working again.

00:10:26.140 --> 00:10:33.290
It's the only way individuals can achieve
their financial objectives, the only way corporations

00:10:33.290 --> 00:10:37.720
will achieve growth, the only way pension
funds will meet their commitments and the

00:10:37.720 --> 00:10:42.840
only way governments can address the great
public policy challenges we face.

00:10:42.840 --> 00:10:48.840
Whether it's funding retirement, supporting
education or rebuilding our infrastructure,

00:10:48.840 --> 00:10:53.620
all of that is really only doable if we reignite
growth.

00:10:53.620 --> 00:11:00.050
So to meet our global challenges in this new
world, we must, at every level, turn savers

00:11:00.050 --> 00:11:04.120
into investors.

00:11:04.120 --> 00:11:06.930
Worry about the future is driving up short-term
savings.

00:11:06.930 --> 00:11:13.310
The FDIC data shows that bank deposits hit
$10 trillion at the -- at year end, up from

00:11:13.310 --> 00:11:15.850
$6 trillion in 2004.

00:11:15.850 --> 00:11:21.970
They grew three times faster in the first
nine months of 2011 than they did in 2010.

00:11:21.970 --> 00:11:26.930
In China, despite strong growth, investors
are keeping far too much of their savings

00:11:26.930 --> 00:11:33.050
in short-term deposits, and if this trend
continues, there will be a need from other

00:11:33.050 --> 00:11:36.390
sectors to finance growth.

00:11:36.390 --> 00:11:41.800
In the same way nonfinancial companies in
the S&amp;P 500 held more than $1 trillion in

00:11:41.800 --> 00:11:46.320
cash as of the fourth quarter of last year,
according to Standard &amp; Poor's -- this is

00:11:46.320 --> 00:11:54.160
the highest percentage of all assets since
the 1960s -- for CEOs sitting on much cash,

00:11:54.160 --> 00:11:57.560
this will ultimately push down their PE ratios.

00:11:57.560 --> 00:11:58.720
So what do we do?

00:11:58.720 --> 00:12:00.580
How do we get all the money working again?

00:12:00.580 --> 00:12:05.180
How do we reignite long-term investment and
growth?

00:12:05.180 --> 00:12:08.950
First we must help investors adapt to the
new world.

00:12:08.950 --> 00:12:13.100
It's in everyone's interest to bolster confidence
in the long-term investments, but I'll be

00:12:13.100 --> 00:12:17.520
the first to say that the asset management
industry has not done a great job in helping

00:12:17.520 --> 00:12:19.970
investors take a long-term view.

00:12:19.970 --> 00:12:23.830
We have to step up to offer guidance and provide
answers.

00:12:23.830 --> 00:12:29.070
To finance longer life spans, we must convince
individuals to start investing now, for the

00:12:29.070 --> 00:12:30.580
long term.

00:12:30.580 --> 00:12:35.790
But longevity should be an asset that can
be levered, not a curse.

00:12:35.790 --> 00:12:39.210
They must understand that there's a cost to
sitting in cash.

00:12:39.210 --> 00:12:40.950
No one talks about that cost.

00:12:40.950 --> 00:12:46.440
They talk about the cost of volatility, they
talk about the cost of making the wrong short-term

00:12:46.440 --> 00:12:54.260
decision, but no one is talking about the
cost of inaction.

00:12:54.260 --> 00:12:58.810
We must understand that sitting in the cash,
even in low inflation -- and we need to focus

00:12:58.810 --> 00:13:04.210
on that for individuals and for society -- people
unable to afford retirement will continue

00:13:04.210 --> 00:13:09.700
to work longer, shifting economic opportunities
for the next generation, pushing unemployment

00:13:09.700 --> 00:13:15.300
rates for younger workers even higher than
they are today.

00:13:15.300 --> 00:13:20.950
We need to educate investors about conforming
-- confronting the growing gap between needs

00:13:20.950 --> 00:13:23.000
and resources for retirement.

00:13:23.000 --> 00:13:29.410
In particular, companies have a moral responsibility
to educate their employees.

00:13:29.410 --> 00:13:34.500
Shifting from a defined benefit plan to a
defined contribution plan doesn't absolve

00:13:34.500 --> 00:13:36.980
them of that responsibility.

00:13:36.980 --> 00:13:42.110
That means getting investors beyond a now
inadequate 60-40 portfolio mix of stocks and

00:13:42.110 --> 00:13:43.190
bonds.

00:13:43.190 --> 00:13:46.980
I personally have said many times I'd be a
hundred percent in equities.

00:13:46.980 --> 00:13:51.850
That fits my risk profile and my views of
the world, though obviously it's not appropriate

00:13:51.850 --> 00:13:53.890
for everyone.

00:13:53.890 --> 00:13:56.610
Most investors need a more diversified portfolio.

00:13:56.610 --> 00:14:00.690
But virtually every investor has to find ways
to achieve a better return than they'll get

00:14:00.690 --> 00:14:05.410
in cash or government bonds for the foreseeable
future.

00:14:05.410 --> 00:14:09.730
Possibilities including looking at new sources
of income, like dividend-paying stocks, higher-yielding

00:14:09.730 --> 00:14:14.630
corporate bonds, using both passive and active
strategies, and alternative investments, which

00:14:14.630 --> 00:14:19.800
are now becoming more widely available for
individuals.

00:14:19.800 --> 00:14:24.200
At the institutional level, we need to help
public and private pension funds face up to

00:14:24.200 --> 00:14:30.720
the adjustments necessary to meet their obligations
to their members, such as revisiting outdated,

00:14:30.720 --> 00:14:34.380
overrestricted investment guidelines.

00:14:34.380 --> 00:14:39.480
The financial community and government can
help -- also help turn savers into investors

00:14:39.480 --> 00:14:45.240
by finding consensus on practical regulation
that increases confidence in our markets.

00:14:45.240 --> 00:14:51.420
With Basel III, banks will be even more conservative
in their lending, and we'll be even more reliant

00:14:51.420 --> 00:14:56.440
on the capital markets in the future.

00:14:56.440 --> 00:15:00.060
So it's vital that the financial community
and governments find consensus on practical

00:15:00.060 --> 00:15:03.240
regulation that promotes confidence in our
markets.

00:15:03.240 --> 00:15:08.390
For example, a new regulation last month by
the CFTC to protect the collateral that customers

00:15:08.390 --> 00:15:13.880
proposed -- when searchly -- when centrally
clearing over-the-counter derivatives will

00:15:13.880 --> 00:15:16.720
promote greater confidence in these markets.

00:15:16.720 --> 00:15:23.440
In the coming months, I also hope that similar
protections will be welcomed for future tradings.

00:15:23.440 --> 00:15:27.000
We need financial products and disclosure
that ensure individual investors know what

00:15:27.000 --> 00:15:31.340
they are buying, including real risks and
real costs.

00:15:31.340 --> 00:15:36.410
We believe, for example, that in the fast-growing
ETF sector, better labeling will boost investor

00:15:36.410 --> 00:15:37.810
confidence.

00:15:37.810 --> 00:15:43.560
We need a tax structure that encourages long-term
growth, including a capital gains tax regime

00:15:43.560 --> 00:15:46.750
that rewards investments over multiple years.

00:15:46.750 --> 00:15:51.190
The holding period for an investment to qualify
for long-term capital gains tax treatment

00:15:51.190 --> 00:15:56.950
should be extended to three years from the
current 12 months, and the rate should decline

00:15:56.950 --> 00:16:00.140
the longer one holds that investment.

00:16:00.140 --> 00:16:04.990
Finally, governments too need to take a longer-term
investment perspective.

00:16:04.990 --> 00:16:09.250
Just as individuals and institutions should
understand that they cannot count on returns

00:16:09.250 --> 00:16:14.820
in one quarter or one year, governments should
consider investments that may not deliver

00:16:14.820 --> 00:16:16.690
returns for years.

00:16:16.690 --> 00:16:21.190
We need government leaders willing to rise
above partisanship and make forward-looking

00:16:21.190 --> 00:16:26.130
investments in crumbling infrastructure and
basic research.

00:16:26.130 --> 00:16:30.980
And probably the most important long-term
investment society can make is in education,

00:16:30.980 --> 00:16:37.530
an antidote to today's short-term preoccupation,
a means to filter the day's headlines and

00:16:37.530 --> 00:16:42.870
see longer-term policies and to adapt to the
competitive demands of the information age,

00:16:42.870 --> 00:16:48.690
where even unskilled positions today require
technology competence.

00:16:48.690 --> 00:16:53.170
The transformational challenges and the resulting
crisis of confidence that we face as a global

00:16:53.170 --> 00:16:55.240
society are daunting.

00:16:55.240 --> 00:16:59.020
But we are better-equipped than at any time
in our history to respond.

00:16:59.020 --> 00:17:05.350
After all, we possess unparalleled knowledge
and experience, financial and planning tools,

00:17:05.350 --> 00:17:11.420
technology and analytics, and we are connected
across the world in ways that were just unimaginable

00:17:11.420 --> 00:17:14.390
a very short period ago.

00:17:14.390 --> 00:17:19.550
In this new world, we can help build -- rebuild
confidence.

00:17:19.550 --> 00:17:24.890
We can rebuild confidence in getting the markets
to moving again, restoring growth and turning

00:17:24.890 --> 00:17:28.230
short-term savers into long-term investors.

00:17:28.230 --> 00:17:33.380
It is our responsibility as leaders of business,
finance and government.

00:17:33.380 --> 00:17:38.050
All of us must come to this -- come to this
answer, and all of us must come to the call

00:17:38.050 --> 00:17:39.400
right now.

00:17:39.400 --> 00:17:41.360
Thank you.

00:17:41.360 --> 00:17:43.320
(Applause.)

00:17:43.320 --> 00:17:49.220
&gt;&gt;BARTIROMO: Thanks, Larry.

00:17:49.220 --> 00:17:57.280
You made great points in terms of broad issues
that we're all living through and facing.

00:17:57.280 --> 00:18:03.530
And before we get into sort of what that means
to us and how to actually approach our financial

00:18:03.530 --> 00:18:07.880
lives as a result of the changes that we're
seeing, let's drill down on a couple of those

00:18:07.880 --> 00:18:10.050
broad issues that you just mentioned.

00:18:10.050 --> 00:18:13.440
And one of them, of course, is the government
deleveraging, governments around the world

00:18:13.440 --> 00:18:15.130
deleveraging.

00:18:15.130 --> 00:18:19.290
This morning, of course, we did get news from
the European Central Bank that the ECB is

00:18:19.290 --> 00:18:23.870
putting forth another lending facility for
the European banks, the LTRO.

00:18:23.870 --> 00:18:25.370
Give us your take on the impact of that.

00:18:25.370 --> 00:18:27.290
Is this going to make a big difference in
Europe?

00:18:27.290 --> 00:18:31.510
&gt;&gt;FINK: In the short run I think it's absolving
much of the problems.

00:18:31.510 --> 00:18:34.400
There are many facets that are going on in
Europe.

00:18:34.400 --> 00:18:38.060
The crisis that we saw last fall was a liquidity
crisis.

00:18:38.060 --> 00:18:42.790
You had the regulators telling banks that
they have to conform to Basel II by this summer,

00:18:42.790 --> 00:18:48.620
and then you have the pending Basel III, which
will be out in 2018 when -- (inaudible).

00:18:48.620 --> 00:18:53.140
And on top of that, you had failing markets.

00:18:53.140 --> 00:18:55.270
Markets were not able to respond.

00:18:55.270 --> 00:18:58.040
And actually, it was the banking system that
was selling assets.

00:18:58.040 --> 00:19:03.480
And so you had a deterioration of all the
sovereign credits that really jeopardized

00:19:03.480 --> 00:19:06.780
the stability of the euro community.

00:19:06.780 --> 00:19:12.640
You had yields over 6 percent for Spain and
Italy.

00:19:12.640 --> 00:19:16.970
This -- and so it was a liquidity crisis that
we experienced in Europe, not unlike the liquidity

00:19:16.970 --> 00:19:19.650
crisis we experienced in the United States.

00:19:19.650 --> 00:19:30.240
By providing this liquidity facility, it stabilizes
liquidity, and you're -- you just don't see

00:19:30.240 --> 00:19:33.180
the pressure from the banking community selling.

00:19:33.180 --> 00:19:36.429
Much of the assets the bank owns are two,
three-year maturity.

00:19:36.429 --> 00:19:40.210
So by providing this lending facility, those
banks don't have to sell.

00:19:40.210 --> 00:19:43.400
They're going to see those loans slowly roll
off.

00:19:43.400 --> 00:19:47.440
And so it does absolve much of the problems
in the short run.

00:19:47.440 --> 00:19:52.740
But I think most importantly, by absolving
the liquidity crisis of Europe, it is now

00:19:52.740 --> 00:19:56.970
giving the new governments of Greece, the
new governments of Italy, the new governments

00:19:56.970 --> 00:20:02.730
of Spain time to fix their political situation
and their economic situation.

00:20:02.730 --> 00:20:06.520
So in Italy and Spain specifically, you have
two new governments who have now publicly

00:20:06.520 --> 00:20:10.430
stated, we're going to manage our deficits
accordingly; we're going to have these austerity

00:20:10.430 --> 00:20:11.530
plans.

00:20:11.530 --> 00:20:13.270
That's only part one.

00:20:13.270 --> 00:20:16.340
Part two, we need growth.

00:20:16.340 --> 00:20:21.080
If we don't have growth in Europe in three
years, this thing all falls apart again.

00:20:21.080 --> 00:20:24.770
So it -- this is not a -- this is not a fix.

00:20:24.770 --> 00:20:32.300
It's -- but it's a stability to give governments
time to work their problems into a more favorable

00:20:32.300 --> 00:20:33.300
position.

00:20:33.300 --> 00:20:37.000
&gt;&gt;BARTIROMO: I feel like part of the reason
that individuals as well as corporations have

00:20:37.000 --> 00:20:40.420
been sitting on cash is because Europe has
been a wild card.

00:20:40.420 --> 00:20:43.860
And we're really not sure how that spills
over onto the United States.

00:20:43.860 --> 00:20:51.540
So as a follow-up, are you more comfortable
today that the European upset is less likely

00:20:51.540 --> 00:20:56.150
to spiral onto the United States and impact
this economy negatively?

00:20:56.150 --> 00:21:04.810
&gt;&gt;FINK: Well, I think for 2012 I think we
are going to see a stable Europe, even with

00:21:04.810 --> 00:21:05.810
Greece.

00:21:05.810 --> 00:21:14.470
I mean, you know, I would not be surprised
to see some event that may force some change

00:21:14.470 --> 00:21:18.210
in the relationship between Greece and Europe.

00:21:18.210 --> 00:21:21.510
There may be some evidence of default sometime
in the future.

00:21:21.510 --> 00:21:27.770
But I believe that result is going to be less
problematic.

00:21:27.770 --> 00:21:31.740
I think -- (inaudible) -- very successful
in stabilizing and firewalling the rest of

00:21:31.740 --> 00:21:37.760
Europe, and therefore, if there was that event
-- and I'm not saying there will be, but if

00:21:37.760 --> 00:21:42.410
there is that type of event, I think Europe
even will be a more stable place.

00:21:42.410 --> 00:21:48.840
So translating that back to our shores, I
don't think Europe is going to be a cause

00:21:48.840 --> 00:21:51.420
of concern for our economy.

00:21:51.420 --> 00:21:59.930
I think the only cause for our economy would
be if, through these facilities, could we

00:21:59.930 --> 00:22:03.110
see a devalued euro.

00:22:03.110 --> 00:22:07.930
Now, the euro has actually rallied because
people are less frightened of the future of

00:22:07.930 --> 00:22:15.210
Europe, but if we had a euro that was pushed
down to 115, to pick a number, that would

00:22:15.210 --> 00:22:18.320
have serious implications for our job growth.

00:22:18.320 --> 00:22:23.540
I mean, we benefited mightily as a country
of having a weakened currency.

00:22:23.540 --> 00:22:28.990
And so I guess I'm not worried about -- I'm
not worried about failure of Europe in 2012;

00:22:28.990 --> 00:22:35.530
I'm worried about success of Europe in 2012
and what does that mean for our shores.

00:22:35.530 --> 00:22:42.700
Out 2014, though, if Europe does not find
sources of growth, of economic growth, then

00:22:42.700 --> 00:22:48.040
we have a much more serious problem ahead
of us, because, you know, they already did

00:22:48.040 --> 00:22:54.440
these lending facilities, and if we still,
after a couple years time, cannot find a mechanism

00:22:54.440 --> 00:23:01.309
for GDP growth, then these countries' ability
to -- you know, to afford these deficits become

00:23:01.309 --> 00:23:02.309
more problematic.

00:23:02.309 --> 00:23:08.640
And so we have two years in front of us to
see if Europe can stabilize its future.

00:23:08.640 --> 00:23:12.070
&gt;&gt;BARTIROMO: And under the umbrella of, OK,
what does this mean to me now and what do

00:23:12.070 --> 00:23:14.990
I do now with my money, you mentioned longevity.

00:23:14.990 --> 00:23:19.059
You know, it seems like every time we discuss
longevity -- I was having a conversation with

00:23:19.059 --> 00:23:22.010
someone in health care recently, and he said,
you know, Marie, we're living longer and we

00:23:22.010 --> 00:23:23.400
are really facing a crisis.

00:23:23.400 --> 00:23:25.680
And I said, whoa, whoa, whoa, stop right there.

00:23:25.680 --> 00:23:27.010
We're facing a crisis?

00:23:27.010 --> 00:23:28.559
Listen to what you just said.

00:23:28.559 --> 00:23:30.370
We're living longer.

00:23:30.370 --> 00:23:31.450
We're living longer.

00:23:31.450 --> 00:23:33.960
This should be a positive, not a crisis.

00:23:33.960 --> 00:23:40.470
Why do you point to that as one of the overall
big issues that we face today, longevity?

00:23:40.470 --> 00:23:45.342
&gt;&gt;FINK: I think we focus way too much on the
directions of the market every day, and as

00:23:45.342 --> 00:23:51.670
I said, people are frightened of, you know,
what that market behavior will do.

00:23:51.670 --> 00:23:56.210
And so we're seeing just many people are frozen.

00:23:56.210 --> 00:24:00.960
No one is asking the question, what is that
cost of doing nothing?

00:24:00.960 --> 00:24:08.360
I don't hear any blog or news station or newspaper
that talks about the cost of doing nothing.

00:24:08.360 --> 00:24:09.970
It is enormous a cost.

00:24:09.970 --> 00:24:14.640
And if you're a 35- or 40-year-old and still
haven't been preparing for your retirement

00:24:14.640 --> 00:24:22.630
in any way, each year goes by, the deficit
or the shortfall that you're going to have

00:24:22.630 --> 00:24:25.130
is huge.

00:24:25.130 --> 00:24:27.810
And so we are going to live longer.

00:24:27.810 --> 00:24:33.920
We have been able to -- lot of science is
-- we've turned a lot of deadly diseases into

00:24:33.920 --> 00:24:35.510
chronic diseases.

00:24:35.510 --> 00:24:44.320
You know, if you are a couple in fairly good
health in your 60s, the statistical probability,

00:24:44.320 --> 00:24:48.450
one of you is going to live to 92 now.

00:24:48.450 --> 00:24:55.290
And, you know, you're going to need an enormous
reservoir of savings to achieve the lifestyle

00:24:55.290 --> 00:24:57.160
or the standard of living that you're looking
for.

00:24:57.160 --> 00:25:01.950
So I think we need to have a call of -- you
know, call to arms right now that we need

00:25:01.950 --> 00:25:04.190
to focus on the costs.

00:25:04.190 --> 00:25:08.710
And we got to do it at the individual level
so it doesn't become a bigger federal burden

00:25:08.710 --> 00:25:09.710
or state burden.

00:25:09.710 --> 00:25:13.460
Obviously, it still remains to be a big state
burden, but I think it's a responsibility

00:25:13.460 --> 00:25:20.610
for every state fund, every corporate plan
to help educate their workers -- and I mean

00:25:20.610 --> 00:25:26.250
part-time workers and full-time workers -- about
what it takes to build the necessary nest

00:25:26.250 --> 00:25:27.250
egg.

00:25:27.250 --> 00:25:31.860
You're not going to be able to rely totally
on Social Security, and so it's going to have

00:25:31.860 --> 00:25:33.960
to be through these private means.

00:25:33.960 --> 00:25:38.630
Other countries in the world have adopted
some very sensible retirement plans, and in

00:25:38.630 --> 00:25:43.701
those countries they've addressed these issues,
like Chile and Australia, where there are

00:25:43.701 --> 00:25:48.730
sensible plans in which people have to save
for retirement.

00:25:48.730 --> 00:25:54.420
&gt;&gt;BARTIROMO: Are you surprised that ever since
the U.S. got downgraded, all this money has

00:25:54.420 --> 00:25:55.640
gone into Treasuries?

00:25:55.640 --> 00:26:01.280
&gt;&gt;FINK: No, because a Treasury is a benchmark,
you know.

00:26:01.280 --> 00:26:08.910
So if you think about a swimming pool and
the volume of water in a swimming pool, and

00:26:08.910 --> 00:26:16.920
that the Treasury component of a portfolio
represents, let's say, 30 percent of that

00:26:16.920 --> 00:26:22.460
volume of water -- and I'm talking about the
volume of water and degrees of risk.

00:26:22.460 --> 00:26:29.760
And so we got downgraded, and so definitionally
now that volume of water of risk increased.

00:26:29.760 --> 00:26:34.851
So Treasuries went from that 25 percent volume
of water to 30 percent of risk because it

00:26:34.851 --> 00:26:37.530
was downgraded.

00:26:37.530 --> 00:26:45.350
So you needed to sell other things if you
wanted to keep your risk appetite the same.

00:26:45.350 --> 00:26:48.020
And so you didn't have to sell Treasuries.

00:26:48.020 --> 00:26:51.340
You -- if you wanted to keep your risk appetite
the same, that means you had to sell other

00:26:51.340 --> 00:26:53.520
things, so Treasuries actually did fine.

00:26:53.520 --> 00:26:58.140
&gt;&gt;BARTIROMO: Give us your sense of the secular
shifts going on in the global economy right

00:26:58.140 --> 00:26:59.140
now.

00:26:59.140 --> 00:27:00.140
You're traveling all over the world.

00:27:00.140 --> 00:27:05.360
We're hearing a lot about Latin America, particularly
Brazil and the vibrancy there.

00:27:05.360 --> 00:27:09.559
We're hearing a lot about, of course, the
growth in China, even if it has slowed down.

00:27:09.559 --> 00:27:13.820
What's your sense how significant is the global
slowdown, China having been the engine of

00:27:13.820 --> 00:27:14.820
growth for the world?

00:27:14.820 --> 00:27:18.720
And what's going on right now in Latin America
or other places you'd like to mention?

00:27:18.720 --> 00:27:21.580
&gt;&gt;FINK: Well, I think Latin America experienced
a slowdown in the third quarter.

00:27:21.580 --> 00:27:27.540
You saw Brazil going from a 5-ish percent
GDP in the first quarter of last year to zero

00:27:27.540 --> 00:27:30.020
and I think in the fourth quarter sort of
turning back up.

00:27:30.020 --> 00:27:34.860
But we've seen a slowdown throughout the world.

00:27:34.860 --> 00:27:37.270
I don't believe China is slowing down in 2012.

00:27:37.270 --> 00:27:39.960
I know I'm in a minority in it.

00:27:39.960 --> 00:27:47.090
I personally believe the present leadership
of China have been very successful.

00:27:47.090 --> 00:27:52.390
I can't imagine them wanting to hand off the
economy to the new leadership with the economy

00:27:52.390 --> 00:27:54.630
in a soft landing or hard landing.

00:27:54.630 --> 00:27:59.110
It doesn't seem that -- you know, you're not
going to want to hand off after having such

00:27:59.110 --> 00:28:01.830
a great tenure of success.

00:28:01.830 --> 00:28:06.730
So I believe you're -- and you're starting
to see evidence of their easing quite a bit

00:28:06.730 --> 00:28:11.080
to assure that the economy grows at 8 1/2-plus
(percent) for 2012.

00:28:11.080 --> 00:28:17.590
In the last few weeks you've seen governments
encouraging banks to roll over debt, and you've

00:28:17.590 --> 00:28:21.570
seen last -- I think it was last week -- the
governments reduce the reserve requirements

00:28:21.570 --> 00:28:22.570
in China.

00:28:22.570 --> 00:28:26.970
So they're actually easing now to restimulate
the economy.

00:28:26.970 --> 00:28:31.440
&gt;&gt;BARTIROMO: So you're worried -- one of your
big concerns is the fact that we are sitting

00:28:31.440 --> 00:28:36.799
on such low-interest-bearing money, and we're
not putting our money to work.

00:28:36.799 --> 00:28:41.240
How are you putting your money to work, and
what are you hearing from the many deep-pocketed

00:28:41.240 --> 00:28:45.320
investors that you're speaking all around
the world in terms of how they're getting

00:28:45.320 --> 00:28:46.320
return?

00:28:46.320 --> 00:28:49.890
&gt;&gt;FINK: Well, we're seeing some very large
flows.

00:28:49.890 --> 00:28:52.190
So we are starting to see some money being
put to work.

00:28:52.190 --> 00:28:58.049
This is obviously why we've had this rally
here today where the NASDAQ is up 15 percent,

00:28:58.049 --> 00:29:01.090
the S&amp;P is up 9 (percent), most global markets
are up 9 percent.

00:29:01.090 --> 00:29:07.690
So you're seeing money starting to be put
to work, and you're seeing the flows are in

00:29:07.690 --> 00:29:08.690
areas like high-yield.

00:29:08.690 --> 00:29:13.200
You're seeing huge flows, like record flows
into high-yield funds here today.

00:29:13.200 --> 00:29:15.780
You're seeing record flows into dividend-oriented
stuff.

00:29:15.780 --> 00:29:22.020
So I think investors are starting to look
at these opportunities.

00:29:22.020 --> 00:29:27.490
We're seeing -- but overall, there's still
this sense of confusion.

00:29:27.490 --> 00:29:33.240
There is still a huge allocation in cash,
huge allocation in bonds.

00:29:33.240 --> 00:29:37.490
So I just think on the margin, people are
putting the money to work.

00:29:37.490 --> 00:29:43.570
We're seeing a -- we're seeing huge reallocation
into alternatives, which actually gives me

00:29:43.570 --> 00:29:51.380
a more constructive view on equities as more
money runs away from equities in some areas

00:29:51.380 --> 00:29:52.580
into alternatives.

00:29:52.580 --> 00:30:00.730
So I -- it really -- investors worldwide are
asking the same question: What should I do?

00:30:00.730 --> 00:30:05.990
And depending on their risk appetite, depending
on their liability needs, you're seeing -- you're

00:30:05.990 --> 00:30:12.380
seeing behavior that is -- it's not behavior
that you could say is uniform.

00:30:12.380 --> 00:30:15.650
It's individual behavior depending on the
needs of each individual investor.

00:30:15.650 --> 00:30:20.050
&gt;&gt;BARTIROMO: So you think that is appropriate
to be looking at dividend payers, to be looking

00:30:20.050 --> 00:30:21.760
at these high corporate bonds?

00:30:21.760 --> 00:30:23.050
Is that what you believe is appropriate?

00:30:23.050 --> 00:30:28.030
&gt;&gt;FINK: Yeah, I mean, if you -- let's talk
about Verizon, a great New York-based company.

00:30:28.030 --> 00:30:31.110
It pays a 5.30 (percent) dividend.

00:30:31.110 --> 00:30:39.220
Their 10-year debt is 3 1/2 percent, 3 (percent),
3 3/4 (percent).

00:30:39.220 --> 00:30:44.549
Even let's talk about a bank that everyone
was worried about only five months ago, Santander,

00:30:44.549 --> 00:30:51.809
big in the United States, big in South America,
big in Great Britain and obviously the largest

00:30:51.809 --> 00:30:53.830
bank in Spain.

00:30:53.830 --> 00:30:55.750
It's paying a 9 percent dividend.

00:30:55.750 --> 00:30:59.820
&gt;&gt;BARTIROMO: Do you think that'll stay like
that, 9 percent?

00:30:59.820 --> 00:31:02.280
&gt;&gt;FINK: Yeah, I do.

00:31:02.280 --> 00:31:06.810
Well, no, it's going to get -- I assume the
stock price will rally, and then dividend

00:31:06.810 --> 00:31:08.990
reprice -- the dividend will be less.

00:31:08.990 --> 00:31:15.419
But I just think there's some great opportunities
where you can buy stocks that are paying you

00:31:15.419 --> 00:31:21.360
a dividend that is far more rewarding than
owning, you know, 10-year U.S. treasuries

00:31:21.360 --> 00:31:22.360
at 1.94 (percent).

00:31:22.360 --> 00:31:27.360
&gt;&gt;BARTIROMO: I want you to expand on one thing
you said in your -- in your remarks, and that

00:31:27.360 --> 00:31:28.460
was about Japan.

00:31:28.460 --> 00:31:34.800
You said we're about to see the Japanese become
-- go from net savers to net sellers of assets.

00:31:34.800 --> 00:31:35.800
What's the impact?

00:31:35.800 --> 00:31:36.870
What are you expecting?

00:31:36.870 --> 00:31:38.134
&gt;&gt;FINK: Well, first of all, it's just age,
you know.

00:31:38.134 --> 00:31:42.030
It's been a great society of savers.

00:31:42.030 --> 00:31:45.342
And over the next 10 years, they're going
to be retiring and they're going to have to

00:31:45.342 --> 00:31:47.130
start spending that retirement.

00:31:47.130 --> 00:31:50.830
And it's just pure math.

00:31:50.830 --> 00:31:53.680
And it -- this should be a wake-up call.

00:31:53.680 --> 00:31:58.240
We have time, but it should be a wake-up in
our country about making sure that we are

00:31:58.240 --> 00:32:00.120
navigating our deficits.

00:32:00.120 --> 00:32:06.470
I think most recent statistics will say that
Japan is the second-largest foreign owner

00:32:06.470 --> 00:32:08.310
of our debt.

00:32:08.310 --> 00:32:12.850
And it's just pure math that will tell you
that ultimately, these -- this nation of savers

00:32:12.850 --> 00:32:20.370
will have to be more -- spending that savings,
and that will reduce their ownership of U.S.

00:32:20.370 --> 00:32:21.370
Treasuries.

00:32:21.370 --> 00:32:24.450
So -- and so it should just be a long-term
wake-up call, and we should be prepared for

00:32:24.450 --> 00:32:25.450
it.

00:32:25.450 --> 00:32:26.559
This is not a train wreck.

00:32:26.559 --> 00:32:29.480
It will be a train wreck if we don't prepare
for it.

00:32:29.480 --> 00:32:34.660
&gt;&gt;BARTIROMO: And amidst all of this, we are
also sort of questioning and debating the

00:32:34.660 --> 00:32:35.660
regulatory environment.

00:32:35.660 --> 00:32:36.660
&gt;&gt;FINK: Yeah.

00:32:36.660 --> 00:32:41.559
&gt;&gt;BARTIROMO: You mentioned in your remarks
the tax structure and some changes that are

00:32:41.559 --> 00:32:42.660
needed there.

00:32:42.660 --> 00:32:45.190
You mentioned Basel III.

00:32:45.190 --> 00:32:50.310
Give us your sense of the regulatory environment,
how it may change.

00:32:50.310 --> 00:32:56.440
And do you believe that the current system
is one of the reasons that companies are sitting

00:32:56.440 --> 00:33:03.610
on cash and unwilling to pick up the hiring
pace?

00:33:03.610 --> 00:33:10.470
&gt;&gt;FINK: Let me answer the latter part first.

00:33:10.470 --> 00:33:17.820
I think -- I think most CEOs want to put that
money to work.

00:33:17.820 --> 00:33:25.790
They know keeping all that money in cash drags
down earnings.

00:33:25.790 --> 00:33:29.740
These are rational men and women who are running
our companies.

00:33:29.740 --> 00:33:34.750
They're basically saying, I -- you know, I
know I'm dragging down my earnings by sitting

00:33:34.750 --> 00:33:42.810
-- keeping all that money in cash, but I'm
not confident of putting that money to work,

00:33:42.810 --> 00:33:47.020
and therefore I'm going to drag down my earnings
with all this money in cash.

00:33:47.020 --> 00:33:51.299
We need to find a way -- and it's -- I don't
think it's Basel III, which I'll talk about

00:33:51.299 --> 00:33:58.250
-- we need to either -- we need to build that
confidence that spending that money is a better

00:33:58.250 --> 00:34:06.059
return on their investment than earning zero,
that spending that money is better for their

00:34:06.059 --> 00:34:07.290
careers.

00:34:07.290 --> 00:34:12.760
We have to all remember, the average CEO in
America has a term of five years.

00:34:12.760 --> 00:34:17.539
And so a lot of times, when you're building
-- doing large capital spending projects,

00:34:17.539 --> 00:34:24.700
it's a drag during their tenure, and the results
may not be achieved until someone else's tenure.

00:34:24.700 --> 00:34:29.750
So you have to feel very confident that -- spending
that money with a short horizon.

00:34:29.750 --> 00:34:32.989
So we -- you know, I didn't say in my speech,
but we should also understand the horizons

00:34:32.989 --> 00:34:40.500
of leadership is shortening now too, and -- which
has an impact on how you spend your money.

00:34:40.500 --> 00:34:44.629
Basel III is just -- you know, to me, Basel
III is no different than Dodd-Frank.

00:34:44.629 --> 00:34:51.379
It is society saying our banking system was
too risky; we need to have buffers so governments

00:34:51.379 --> 00:34:57.250
don't have to stand in between the failure
of a bank and society.

00:34:57.250 --> 00:34:59.950
There's cost associated with that.

00:34:59.950 --> 00:35:05.749
So the men and women who run banks worldwide
-- they're rational people.

00:35:05.749 --> 00:35:11.630
Their capital has gone up; therefore their
cost -- their need to make a return on equity

00:35:11.630 --> 00:35:14.940
has to go up, because they have more equity
capital.

00:35:14.940 --> 00:35:21.079
And so by definition, they're going to have
to charge more for their services.

00:35:21.079 --> 00:35:25.619
So that means all of us -- all the pension
funds, all the investors -- we have added

00:35:25.619 --> 00:35:26.869
cost.

00:35:26.869 --> 00:35:32.720
So the -- so if we don't use our capital markets
and enlarge our capital markets, you know,

00:35:32.720 --> 00:35:36.220
the banks are going to charge you more for
their services to make the appropriate return

00:35:36.220 --> 00:35:38.930
on equity that shareholders demand.

00:35:38.930 --> 00:35:41.670
And that's what -- that's what society wanted
with Basel III.

00:35:41.670 --> 00:35:44.790
That's what society wanted with Dodd-Frank.

00:35:44.790 --> 00:35:50.470
And so this is -- so for us to reduce those
added costs, we need to have a more fulsome

00:35:50.470 --> 00:35:58.210
global capital market, so corporations can
fund and governments can fund and grow.

00:35:58.210 --> 00:36:03.520
&gt;&gt;BARTIROMO: Are you expecting the Volcker
rule to be implemented in July?

00:36:03.520 --> 00:36:05.390
(Laughter.)

00:36:05.390 --> 00:36:13.049
&gt;&gt;FINK: I think some form of the Volcker rule
will be implemented.

00:36:13.049 --> 00:36:20.130
I think the Volcker rule has some holes in
it.

00:36:20.130 --> 00:36:21.630
We are not in support of it.

00:36:21.630 --> 00:36:25.470
We sent the letter as a firm.

00:36:25.470 --> 00:36:29.779
It's very hard for me to understand how to
navigate the Volcker rule.

00:36:29.779 --> 00:36:31.579
What is proprietary trading?

00:36:31.579 --> 00:36:32.579
What is flow trading?

00:36:32.579 --> 00:36:34.849
It's going to be very definitional.

00:36:34.849 --> 00:36:41.140
But I do believe the Volcker rule gives the
Federal Reserve a lot of latitude.

00:36:41.140 --> 00:36:46.580
And so I do believe the Volcker rule will
be effective in July.

00:36:46.580 --> 00:36:50.089
We won't know how it's going to be manifested
by the Federal Reserve.

00:36:50.089 --> 00:36:55.609
How will they interpret flow trading versus
proprietary?

00:36:55.609 --> 00:37:02.589
So if a securities firm, a bank buys a bond
from BlackRock or -- and they can't readily

00:37:02.589 --> 00:37:07.349
sell it, but they sell another instrument
to hedge it, definitionally will that be called

00:37:07.349 --> 00:37:08.599
flow trading?

00:37:08.599 --> 00:37:12.520
Or definitionally will that now be called
proprietary trading -- therefore, it's an

00:37:12.520 --> 00:37:13.790
illegal action?

00:37:13.790 --> 00:37:18.030
And so it's going to be up to a huge amount
of interpretation.

00:37:18.030 --> 00:37:22.960
And this is why it's so confusing, and this
is why I think it's so debatable, because

00:37:22.960 --> 00:37:28.339
it's very -- you know, it's very hard to understand
how will the Federal Reserve navigate this.

00:37:28.339 --> 00:37:32.609
&gt;&gt;BARTIROMO: And you're -- and you're saying,
look, you need to be active and not passive.

00:37:32.609 --> 00:37:36.309
And you need to control your money and make
your money work for you because the forces

00:37:36.309 --> 00:37:41.500
around us are still in place, whether it be
this uncertainty around regulation, whether

00:37:41.500 --> 00:37:46.869
it be these low-yield environments, whether
it be the pressures from longevity or whatever

00:37:46.869 --> 00:37:47.869
else.

00:37:47.869 --> 00:37:52.660
So tell us your plan, in terms of navigating
all of these issues?

00:37:52.660 --> 00:37:57.680
In straightforward way, what are you doing
as the largest asset manager today?

00:37:57.680 --> 00:38:02.970
&gt;&gt;FINK: What I try to tell all our investors
at BlackRock is, one, try to filter out all

00:38:02.970 --> 00:38:04.599
the noise.

00:38:04.599 --> 00:38:08.450
We are going to have more regulation of some
sort.

00:38:08.450 --> 00:38:12.400
We're going to have more uncertainty.

00:38:12.400 --> 00:38:14.839
We're going to have issues around Europe or
China.

00:38:14.839 --> 00:38:18.859
We're going to have elections in France in
May, which will have a pronounced impact on

00:38:18.859 --> 00:38:19.940
where Europe goes.

00:38:19.940 --> 00:38:22.470
We're going to have elections here in this
country.

00:38:22.470 --> 00:38:27.050
So we have all these different issues.

00:38:27.050 --> 00:38:31.930
But inaction is just the wrong decision.

00:38:31.930 --> 00:38:38.289
And as I said earlier, the sooner you start
putting that money to work to earn 5, 6, 7

00:38:38.289 --> 00:38:48.369
percent returns, the sooner you start navigating
a horizon beyond 10 years for retirement.

00:38:48.369 --> 00:38:51.890
You can't focus on the minute.

00:38:51.890 --> 00:38:52.900
You can't focus on the noise.

00:38:52.900 --> 00:38:57.089
Now, I'm not trying to suggest we could have
-- you know, we may have the market fall back

00:38:57.089 --> 00:38:59.960
another -- you know, back to where it was
at the beginning of year, so I'm not trying

00:38:59.960 --> 00:39:03.369
to suggest when or where the entry level is.

00:39:03.369 --> 00:39:11.489
But the longer you have a view over a long
horizon to meet a target -- and this is really

00:39:11.489 --> 00:39:18.020
critical -- the longer you have this view
and a need -- so if earning 5 percent is the

00:39:18.020 --> 00:39:23.970
right amount of return you need to get to
your nest egg that you need, it really doesn't

00:39:23.970 --> 00:39:26.329
matter, your entry level.

00:39:26.329 --> 00:39:32.580
And so I'm trying to get around day trading,
trying to getting around the notion of, when

00:39:32.580 --> 00:39:35.380
should I jump in?

00:39:35.380 --> 00:39:37.079
The markets are going to move up and down.

00:39:37.079 --> 00:39:39.160
&gt;&gt;BARTIROMO: Don't time the market.

00:39:39.160 --> 00:39:40.499
&gt;&gt;FINK: Yeah.

00:39:40.499 --> 00:39:45.549
And yet, you know, we have, you know, hundreds
and hundreds of people talking about market

00:39:45.549 --> 00:39:48.289
timing and when to get in and when to get
out.

00:39:48.289 --> 00:39:51.050
And that to me is a noise that we have to
remove.

00:39:51.050 --> 00:39:56.420
We have to ask ourself each individually,
each company, what is right for you?

00:39:56.420 --> 00:39:58.019
What do you need?

00:39:58.019 --> 00:40:03.670
Some person who has a lower standard of living
need may only need a 4 percent return.

00:40:03.670 --> 00:40:07.529
I could live with that $1,200 a month of monthly
income.

00:40:07.529 --> 00:40:09.710
I could live very successfully.

00:40:09.710 --> 00:40:13.819
Another person would say, gosh, I need 3,000
(dollars) a month or 4,000 (dollars) a month.

00:40:13.819 --> 00:40:15.670
And so you have to work backwards.

00:40:15.670 --> 00:40:20.130
Now, obviously, inflation will erode that
future value, and so you have to add some

00:40:20.130 --> 00:40:22.430
type of inflation quotient in there.

00:40:22.430 --> 00:40:27.520
But I really do believe we need to start asking
these easier questions about, what do you

00:40:27.520 --> 00:40:31.549
believe you need to have the standard of living
you need to?

00:40:31.549 --> 00:40:34.559
And stop focusing on the noise.

00:40:34.559 --> 00:40:36.369
The noise is confusing.

00:40:36.369 --> 00:40:41.769
And in most cases, the noise, in my opinion,
is a healer.

00:40:41.769 --> 00:40:45.310
The noise is not -- should not be considered
destabilizing.

00:40:45.310 --> 00:40:51.050
The noise, in our democratic society, creates
movement, and most of the time the noise actually

00:40:51.050 --> 00:40:52.979
resolve(s) most problems.

00:40:52.979 --> 00:40:55.410
&gt;&gt;BARTIROMO: And there's a lot of -- and then
a lot of noise out there.

00:40:55.410 --> 00:40:56.999
We're going to open it up to the floor.

00:40:56.999 --> 00:40:58.680
We want to hear from our members.

00:40:58.680 --> 00:41:04.170
But before I do that, the NASDAQ being up
15 percent and the 9 percent on most major

00:41:04.170 --> 00:41:09.839
averages, is that appropriate, in your view,
given the economic backdrop in the United

00:41:09.839 --> 00:41:10.839
States?

00:41:10.839 --> 00:41:15.999
&gt;&gt;FINK: Well, if you just answer the -- in
the -- if you put it in the context of where

00:41:15.999 --> 00:41:23.009
we had in the last two years, it's -- we've
had so much derisking going on that you're

00:41:23.009 --> 00:41:25.619
just seeing now people going back into the
market.

00:41:25.619 --> 00:41:32.809
Let me just say one last point about why I
think it is appropriate.

00:41:32.809 --> 00:41:40.069
Because of the credit crisis, most financial
institutions have shortened up the maturity

00:41:40.069 --> 00:41:41.799
of their assets.

00:41:41.799 --> 00:41:44.980
Most people don't talk about this.

00:41:44.980 --> 00:41:49.359
But at the BlackRock Investment Institute,
we calculated insurance companies to have

00:41:49.359 --> 00:41:52.690
$600 billion of purchasing power this year.

00:41:52.690 --> 00:41:56.300
That means they have $600 billion of assets
that are rolling over.

00:41:56.300 --> 00:41:58.059
I'm not even talking about the banks.

00:41:58.059 --> 00:42:03.960
So you add up, just because of shortened duration
of portfolios, and the rollover of insurance

00:42:03.960 --> 00:42:08.249
companies, the rollover of banks that need
to put money to work, on top of all the cash

00:42:08.249 --> 00:42:15.230
that's sitting in corporations, all the other
cash that's sitting around, you know, I still

00:42:15.230 --> 00:42:18.969
believe the marketplaces have a lot more movement
upward.

00:42:18.969 --> 00:42:26.190
And also, I believe this answers the question
why rates are staying so low, because the

00:42:26.190 --> 00:42:30.039
amount of money that's sitting there is so
enormous.

00:42:30.039 --> 00:42:35.020
So if you add up this continuation of deleveraging
that's happening in our country, even with

00:42:35.020 --> 00:42:39.430
the federal deficits, we're going to have
$200 billion less outstanding debt as a nation.

00:42:39.430 --> 00:42:41.650
Last year we had $300 billion less debt.

00:42:41.650 --> 00:42:45.950
So we are seeing this deleveraging happening
as a society at the same -- you know, so at

00:42:45.950 --> 00:42:52.250
the private level, we're seeing a huge deleveraging
as the public sector deficits are $1.2 trillion.

00:42:52.250 --> 00:42:57.979
So even with this $1.2 trillion deficit, we
are seeing less outstanding debt as a society.

00:42:57.979 --> 00:43:02.380
So you factor in the less outstanding debt
as a society, coupled with a short duration

00:43:02.380 --> 00:43:08.059
of maturity that banks and insurance companies
-- it doesn't surprise me that we continue

00:43:08.059 --> 00:43:09.539
to have low rates.

00:43:09.539 --> 00:43:14.400
We're going to continue to have low rates,
which from my perspective gives me a foundation

00:43:14.400 --> 00:43:17.599
to believe that equities are going to be a
great place to be.

00:43:17.599 --> 00:43:19.400
&gt;&gt;BARTIROMO: Question from the audience.

00:43:19.400 --> 00:43:20.400
Dan?

00:43:20.400 --> 00:43:23.369
&gt;&gt;QUESTIONER: Good morning, Larry.

00:43:23.369 --> 00:43:26.569
Daniel Arbess from Perella Weinberg Partners.

00:43:26.569 --> 00:43:35.099
Your observations about investors not achieving
their return hurdles are, of course, of great

00:43:35.099 --> 00:43:36.099
concern.

00:43:36.099 --> 00:43:42.700
We're in a global debt deleveraging, where
central banks are effectively practicing financial

00:43:42.700 --> 00:43:48.450
repression on all kinds of savers, pushing
people into riskier assets.

00:43:48.450 --> 00:43:54.550
My question follows Maria's last question,
which is, the notion that you can get a higher

00:43:54.550 --> 00:44:00.839
return on equities is an interesting notion,
but we all know that equities are much riskier,

00:44:00.839 --> 00:44:02.219
as well.

00:44:02.219 --> 00:44:08.420
So the environment that we're in, characterized
by a lot of contentiousness around austerity,

00:44:08.420 --> 00:44:13.150
taxation, et cetera, et cetera, is not really
conducive to robust economic growth.

00:44:13.150 --> 00:44:18.359
At what point do you say equity valuations
are going to be stretched so that there's

00:44:18.359 --> 00:44:22.469
as much risk as there is potential enhancement
and return going into the asset class?

00:44:22.469 --> 00:44:28.490
&gt;&gt;FINK: So I would argue that, you know, equities,
despite this rally, are unchanged in 10 years.

00:44:28.490 --> 00:44:35.489
We have P/E ratios that are hovering around
in the 12s.

00:44:35.489 --> 00:44:40.520
You know, we had equity markets hovering around
the 12s in 1980, too.

00:44:40.520 --> 00:44:45.960
We had 10-year Treasuries at 9 and 10 percent,
and now we have 10-year Treasuries at 2 percent.

00:44:45.960 --> 00:44:52.489
I think we have a long ways to go to make
equities look expensive.

00:44:52.489 --> 00:44:58.010
And I do believe our corporations are in unbelievable
shape.

00:44:58.010 --> 00:45:01.270
Their balance sheets have never been stronger,
certainly a lot stronger today than they were

00:45:01.270 --> 00:45:07.140
in 1980, certainly their ability to navigate
with low interest rates really allows them

00:45:07.140 --> 00:45:10.789
to do things they would never have been able
to do.

00:45:10.789 --> 00:45:20.579
And so at this time, looking at long-term
valuations, I think equities are the place

00:45:20.579 --> 00:45:21.579
to be.

00:45:21.579 --> 00:45:23.269
Now, that's my personal view.

00:45:23.269 --> 00:45:28.670
And, you know, I believe you're paid to own
equities, especially when you incorporate

00:45:28.670 --> 00:45:32.460
not only the P/E ratios but the dividend returns
that you're getting in equities.

00:45:32.460 --> 00:45:35.700
&gt;&gt;BARTIROMO: And that is always U.S. equities?

00:45:35.700 --> 00:45:39.260
Are you more poised to put money in the U.S.
right now or outside the U.S.?

00:45:39.260 --> 00:45:43.499
&gt;&gt;FINK: I would say what I personally like
the most is buying multinational companies

00:45:43.499 --> 00:45:45.989
that are less dependent on one country.

00:45:45.989 --> 00:45:49.309
I like multinational companies.

00:45:49.309 --> 00:45:55.089
They may have 40 to 60 percent of their engines
of growth in the United States, but I do like

00:45:55.089 --> 00:45:57.140
the diversification of being more global.

00:45:57.140 --> 00:46:02.410
&gt;&gt;BARTIROMO: We've got a caller coming in,
national member, Ben Wolf from Celfin Capital,

00:46:02.410 --> 00:46:07.309
in Santiago, Chile, right now, asking: You
mentioned the success of the Chilean privatized

00:46:07.309 --> 00:46:11.499
pension fund system, which has not only provided
an excellent retirement system but has also

00:46:11.499 --> 00:46:15.950
contributed significantly to the development
of mature and deep capital markets, which

00:46:15.950 --> 00:46:17.430
in turn fuel growth.

00:46:17.430 --> 00:46:21.299
Do you think there is any prospect that the
U.S. or any other advanced economy adopts

00:46:21.299 --> 00:46:26.259
a similar system either in whole or in part
in the medium term?

00:46:26.259 --> 00:46:27.829
&gt;&gt;FINK: I hope so.

00:46:27.829 --> 00:46:32.730
I think as we -- I said earlier, if you look
at Chile or Australia, they're going to have

00:46:32.730 --> 00:46:43.219
a pool of resources to provide the capital
to build infrastructure, to build -- to build

00:46:43.219 --> 00:46:45.710
these countries.

00:46:45.710 --> 00:46:51.869
You know, when we tried to do that I think
three, four years ago, they called it -- when

00:46:51.869 --> 00:46:57.369
I think Secretary Paulson was talking about
the privatization of Social Security, that's

00:46:57.369 --> 00:47:05.950
basically what they were trying to do, creating
a mechanism in which investors had choice.

00:47:05.950 --> 00:47:10.089
In Australia, you have the superannuation
funds in which you either -- you have eight

00:47:10.089 --> 00:47:15.529
or nine options how to invest, and you choose
how to invest.

00:47:15.529 --> 00:47:22.489
But it's a government requirement for part-time
and full-time to withhold 12 percent of their

00:47:22.489 --> 00:47:23.489
disposable income.

00:47:23.489 --> 00:47:27.210
Now, if we did this in this country, we'd
go -- we could go right into a recession.

00:47:27.210 --> 00:47:32.050
You know, and so there's -- there has to be
this balance of how much money are you putting

00:47:32.050 --> 00:47:37.079
away, and how much money are you -- are you
using for consumption.

00:47:37.079 --> 00:47:43.589
So it's not an easy thing to go from a nation
that is probably only putting, you know, 6

00:47:43.589 --> 00:47:48.829
(percent) or 7 percent away, which is inadequate
right now, where you really don't control

00:47:48.829 --> 00:47:50.630
it in the DB side.

00:47:50.630 --> 00:47:54.079
On the DC side, you have choice.

00:47:54.079 --> 00:48:00.809
But you know, as I said, in these other countries,
you have fewer choices to go into -- you know,

00:48:00.809 --> 00:48:03.150
different structures that help the individuals.

00:48:03.150 --> 00:48:05.319
I've seen in Australia how it's been done.

00:48:05.319 --> 00:48:08.690
The individuals are actually -- see if you
save this much money, here's what you're going

00:48:08.690 --> 00:48:12.209
to be having at the end of the -- and you
know, when you retire; this is how much money

00:48:12.209 --> 00:48:14.480
you could have at 65 or 68 or 70.

00:48:14.480 --> 00:48:17.200
And it gives -- it gives you a lot more clarity.

00:48:17.200 --> 00:48:22.430
&gt;&gt;BARTIROMO: Well, it's sort of mapping it
out, mapping out the plan.

00:48:22.430 --> 00:48:24.789
Yes, right there.

00:48:24.789 --> 00:48:27.069
&gt;&gt;QUESTIONER: I'm Susan Davis.

00:48:27.069 --> 00:48:29.759
I lead a nonprofit organization called BRAC.

00:48:29.759 --> 00:48:35.359
And I was intrigued by your perspective or
call to arms for us to take a longer-term

00:48:35.359 --> 00:48:39.619
perspective, investing in infrastructure and
education.

00:48:39.619 --> 00:48:45.530
And I wondered, is there a -- in looking at
equities, what's your firm's perspective on

00:48:45.530 --> 00:48:47.469
impact investing?

00:48:47.469 --> 00:48:56.309
And in the context of this systemic failure,
do you look at equities or companies -- business

00:48:56.309 --> 00:49:04.109
as usual, or are you factoring in the climate
change crisis, global poverty, the tensions

00:49:04.109 --> 00:49:07.440
that are causing the Arab Spring and other
factors?

00:49:07.440 --> 00:49:10.869
&gt;&gt;FINK: (Chuckles.)

00:49:10.869 --> 00:49:12.589
Yes.

00:49:12.589 --> 00:49:14.299
(Laughter.)

00:49:14.299 --> 00:49:19.809
No -- all these social issues are -- you know,
are very difficult to navigate.

00:49:19.809 --> 00:49:24.670
Depending on each individual company, you
have to -- you have to pay attention to these

00:49:24.670 --> 00:49:27.780
issues.

00:49:27.780 --> 00:49:32.269
There's no question that we all have a responsibility.

00:49:32.269 --> 00:49:35.000
We have a responsibility to our country.

00:49:35.000 --> 00:49:39.049
We have a responsibility to certainly our
employees.

00:49:39.049 --> 00:49:46.749
We have a responsibility to our community
and to -- and navigating those issues are

00:49:46.749 --> 00:49:48.920
difficult.

00:49:48.920 --> 00:49:57.640
The pressures of earnings is one that's very
hard for a lot of companies.

00:49:57.640 --> 00:50:01.469
But getting back to me as an investor or how
we -- as BlackRock as an investor in terms

00:50:01.469 --> 00:50:07.460
of how we look at these things, we look at
impact investing; we look at social issues

00:50:07.460 --> 00:50:12.460
as a very important component, especially
as we think about proxy voting.

00:50:12.460 --> 00:50:19.890
You know, we just sent out a letter under
my -- under my name to 600 companies worldwide

00:50:19.890 --> 00:50:23.109
about proxy season.

00:50:23.109 --> 00:50:32.079
And as a shareholder, we are taking a much
more -- interest in how companies perform.

00:50:32.079 --> 00:50:38.390
And we believe it is our fiduciary responsibility
to take an active view, especially in light

00:50:38.390 --> 00:50:40.569
of so much of our assets are indexed money.

00:50:40.569 --> 00:50:42.260
We have to own these companies.

00:50:42.260 --> 00:50:46.150
Whether we like them or not, whether we think
they're doing the right thing or not, we have

00:50:46.150 --> 00:50:47.470
to own them.

00:50:47.470 --> 00:50:51.779
And so therefore the only power we have is
our vote.

00:50:51.779 --> 00:50:57.369
And we're spending a great time now -- which
I'm not involved in; I can't be involved at

00:50:57.369 --> 00:50:58.369
all.

00:50:58.369 --> 00:51:03.430
We have a special committee that is involved
in terms of making those decisions on issues

00:51:03.430 --> 00:51:07.599
that you brought up and how that impacts our
voting.

00:51:07.599 --> 00:51:14.119
And what we wanted to do through this letter,
to tell all our companies that we invest our

00:51:14.119 --> 00:51:18.411
monies with, our clients' money with, that
our vote is going to be important, and we

00:51:18.411 --> 00:51:25.000
are not going to allow our votes to be influenced
by proxy voting services, which I do believe

00:51:25.000 --> 00:51:31.410
-- too many investors rely on proxy voting
services and not spend enough time on taking

00:51:31.410 --> 00:51:35.140
their own responsibility of voting on behalf
of their investors.

00:51:35.140 --> 00:51:40.299
So I don't have a straightforward answer,
but I can tell you we are taking a pretty

00:51:40.299 --> 00:51:49.539
deliberate view on these social issues, and
we will express that one by one in the proxy

00:51:49.539 --> 00:51:50.569
voting season.

00:51:50.569 --> 00:51:54.410
&gt;&gt;BARTIROMO: And one of the priorities that
you've been mentioning this morning is a return

00:51:54.410 --> 00:52:01.239
to growth, reigniting growth, but we walk
such a balance in terms of reigniting growth

00:52:01.239 --> 00:52:04.099
and putting austerity in place, even in the
United States.

00:52:04.099 --> 00:52:08.829
How worried are you about the $14 trillion
debt in the United States?

00:52:08.829 --> 00:52:15.880
Should we be prioritizing cutting back, putting
austerity in more so than we are?

00:52:15.880 --> 00:52:23.140
Or is putting enough stimulus out there to
reignite growth really the preferred priority?

00:52:23.140 --> 00:52:28.630
&gt;&gt;FINK: Well, that's the big debate today,
about how much stimulus we need to re-engage

00:52:28.630 --> 00:52:30.150
this economy.

00:52:30.150 --> 00:52:36.450
Of course we worry about the $14 trillion
of debt, and the debt's growing a trillion-plus

00:52:36.450 --> 00:52:39.579
(dollars) this coming year and probably another
trillion dollars next year.

00:52:39.579 --> 00:52:43.539
So we have not -- you know, despite all this
noise, we have not addressed our deficits

00:52:43.539 --> 00:52:45.380
at all as a country.

00:52:45.380 --> 00:52:50.980
We -- you know, there's been very little in
terms of bipartisanship in terms of navigating

00:52:50.980 --> 00:52:54.470
these deficits, and that's going to be a priority
for 2013.

00:52:54.470 --> 00:52:57.450
&gt;&gt;BARTIROMO: Can the U.S. turn into Italy?

00:52:57.450 --> 00:53:02.079
&gt;&gt;FINK: No, because Italy actually has more
savings than us -- (chuckles) -- plus the

00:53:02.079 --> 00:53:03.079
--

00:53:03.079 --> 00:53:04.079
(Laughter.)

00:53:04.079 --> 00:53:07.079
&gt;&gt;BARTIROMO: But I mean, you're talking about
120 percent debt to GDP -- I mean, everybody's

00:53:07.079 --> 00:53:08.079
trying to make a comparison --

00:53:08.079 --> 00:53:11.230
&gt;&gt;FINK: Yeah, from -- but they actually have
so much savings there that, you know, the

00:53:11.230 --> 00:53:14.640
difference between -- you know, the difference
between Italy and the United States -- 80,

00:53:14.640 --> 00:53:18.760
90 percent -- I don't know the exact statistic,
but a high percentage of Italian government

00:53:18.760 --> 00:53:21.170
debt is held by Italians.

00:53:21.170 --> 00:53:22.170
We don't have that problem.

00:53:22.170 --> 00:53:25.839
We have -- as I said, we have Japanese and
Chinese and a lot of foreigners owning our

00:53:25.839 --> 00:53:26.999
debt.

00:53:26.999 --> 00:53:31.660
And so, you know, we have -- we have a problem.

00:53:31.660 --> 00:53:33.390
As I said, it's not a train wreck problem.

00:53:33.390 --> 00:53:34.910
It's not something happening now.

00:53:34.910 --> 00:53:38.559
It's a problem that you could actually, you
know, look out and --

00:53:38.559 --> 00:53:39.559
&gt;&gt;BARTIROMO: A plan --

00:53:39.559 --> 00:53:43.200
&gt;&gt;FINK: -- if you don't address this in the
next few years, it's going to be a hole that

00:53:43.200 --> 00:53:45.009
is going to be very hard to navigate.

00:53:45.009 --> 00:53:47.739
&gt;&gt;BARTIROMO: Jacob Frenkel.

00:53:47.739 --> 00:53:52.289
&gt;&gt;QUESTIONER: (Audio break) -- thank you.

00:53:52.289 --> 00:53:55.029
Jacob Frenkel, JPMorgan.

00:53:55.029 --> 00:54:02.259
You described the global deleveraging like
-- as the major -- as one of the major dangers

00:54:02.259 --> 00:54:03.369
that we have.

00:54:03.369 --> 00:54:10.819
And indeed governments all over the world
have tried to mitigate the consequence of

00:54:10.819 --> 00:54:17.200
the global deleverage of the private sector
by creating excess leverage of their own books,

00:54:17.200 --> 00:54:21.509
and that's the budget deficits and the debt,
and central banks have expanded their balance

00:54:21.509 --> 00:54:23.319
sheet in an unreasonable way.

00:54:23.319 --> 00:54:30.390
So question number one: Is it obvious that
excess leverage of the public sector is better

00:54:30.390 --> 00:54:35.039
than the excess leverage of the private sector,
which it is supposed to replace?

00:54:35.039 --> 00:54:40.910
And the second question related to it is,
given the extraordinary low interest rate,

00:54:40.910 --> 00:54:46.099
which you spoke -- as a challenge to private
investor, is there also a big challenge to

00:54:46.099 --> 00:54:48.630
the macroeconomy of negative real rates?

00:54:48.630 --> 00:54:55.640
Do we plant the seeds of the bubbles and the
next inflation?

00:54:55.640 --> 00:55:02.709
&gt;&gt;FINK: I don't see any -- let me go from
the back first, Jacob.

00:55:02.709 --> 00:55:07.239
I don't see any risk of inflation for the
next few years.

00:55:07.239 --> 00:55:11.900
Until we start seeing some real growth in
employment in Europe and the United States

00:55:11.900 --> 00:55:16.349
and more stable, growing economies, I don't
think we're going to have that much inflation.

00:55:16.349 --> 00:55:20.989
Now we may have spiked inflation if there's
any crisis in the Middle East related to oil

00:55:20.989 --> 00:55:22.650
and things like that.

00:55:22.650 --> 00:55:29.699
But I'm not -- and I do believe our central
banks have time to navigate a change of policy

00:55:29.699 --> 00:55:35.869
if there was any (whiff/width ?) of employment,
which would translate into an increase in

00:55:35.869 --> 00:55:37.490
inflation.

00:55:37.490 --> 00:55:41.550
So I don't see an inflation bubble in the
next one or two, three years.

00:55:41.550 --> 00:55:44.199
I think there is time for that.

00:55:44.199 --> 00:55:51.890
(Audio break) -- one can argue -- (audio break)
-- we had proper regulation, having the leverage

00:55:51.890 --> 00:55:57.799
in the private sector is probably more sensible,
because we have shareholders who attack it

00:55:57.799 --> 00:56:02.680
every moment, whereby it's very hard to attack
government.

00:56:02.680 --> 00:56:13.549
And so one can argue having this increase
in deficits in the public sector is more difficult

00:56:13.549 --> 00:56:15.569
to navigate, outwardly.

00:56:15.569 --> 00:56:25.160
On the other hand, there is a role of the
public sector to try to stimulate economies

00:56:25.160 --> 00:56:31.719
and to try to create a(n) environment where
our economies can grow.

00:56:31.719 --> 00:56:34.760
So I believe it's a balancing between the
public sector and the private sector.

00:56:34.760 --> 00:56:40.349
Now, obviously, you're correct in saying all
the growth in the deficits are in the public

00:56:40.349 --> 00:56:45.650
sector, whether it is in the balance sheets
of a government or in their Federal Reserves

00:56:45.650 --> 00:56:49.539
or central banks.

00:56:49.539 --> 00:56:57.150
I do believe there is -- we have time to navigate
those risks if it's done sensibly.

00:56:57.150 --> 00:57:05.709
But I do believe we need to really make sure
that we are -- that we have a system in which

00:57:05.709 --> 00:57:12.109
we don't have crises like we had in 2008 and
2009.

00:57:12.109 --> 00:57:18.309
But I don't think we understand the cost of
those mechanisms.

00:57:18.309 --> 00:57:23.829
And so I do believe we're all going to have
to be living in a future with a more regulated

00:57:23.829 --> 00:57:25.229
approach.

00:57:25.229 --> 00:57:30.469
And if we don't develop our capital markets
-- not just here in the United States, but

00:57:30.469 --> 00:57:35.819
if Asia doesn't develop their capital markets,
their problems will be just as severe as our

00:57:35.819 --> 00:57:37.089
problems in the future.

00:57:37.089 --> 00:57:38.809
&gt;&gt;BARTIROMO: We want to have one more question.

00:57:38.809 --> 00:57:40.839
I -- all the way in the back, right over there.

00:57:40.839 --> 00:57:46.279
We want to keep everybody on time, so briefly
want -- actually it was the person in the

00:57:46.279 --> 00:57:47.279
back.

00:57:47.279 --> 00:57:48.279
MS.

00:57:48.279 --> 00:57:49.279
: Very back.

00:57:49.279 --> 00:57:50.279
&gt;&gt;BARTIROMO: Sorry.

00:57:50.279 --> 00:57:52.069
&gt;&gt;QUESTIONER: I'm Rob Dieterich from Bloomberg
Markets Magazine.

00:57:52.069 --> 00:57:56.430
I just want -- earlier you said -- you made
a direct connection between the current crisis

00:57:56.430 --> 00:58:02.520
of confidence or lack of confidence and our
inability to make long-term decisions and

00:58:02.520 --> 00:58:04.369
sort of long-term thinking, I believe you
were saying.

00:58:04.369 --> 00:58:07.510
But I want to sort of challenge that or ask
what the connection you're making is.

00:58:07.510 --> 00:58:12.660
If you look back to, say, circa 2007, when
I think we probably arguably had an excess

00:58:12.660 --> 00:58:17.969
of confidence in the system, I don't think
we were making long-term investment -- (inaudible)

00:58:17.969 --> 00:58:20.079
-- decisions at that point either.

00:58:20.079 --> 00:58:23.859
So what's the connection you're trying to
make between the current crisis of confidence

00:58:23.859 --> 00:58:29.440
and our inabilities or our problems right
now to make long-term thinking?

00:58:29.440 --> 00:58:48.479
&gt;&gt;FINK: Well, I think -- in 2007, we had certainly
over -- we had -- we had confidence that was

00:58:48.479 --> 00:58:56.009
obviously, as you said, way over our (skis
?). We had -- we believed that leverage is

00:58:56.009 --> 00:59:00.510
not a pernicious problem in society.

00:59:00.510 --> 00:59:09.130
So that overconfidence was that we believed,
you know, a couple dozen men and women could

00:59:09.130 --> 00:59:15.660
navigate credit risk in 30- and 40-1 (leverage
?).

00:59:15.660 --> 00:59:19.700
Unfortunately they were wrong, and regulators
were there too.

00:59:19.700 --> 00:59:24.410
So it's not like -- and shareholders were
there too, I mean, nothing was not -- let

00:59:24.410 --> 00:59:26.520
me restate that: Reporters were there too.

00:59:26.520 --> 00:59:33.699
So -- (laughter) -- we were all guilty of
overconfident that we could navigate that

00:59:33.699 --> 00:59:36.249
type of risk.

00:59:36.249 --> 00:59:44.529
But I think it's the backlash -- the other
side of that problem is the -- is the causal

00:59:44.529 --> 00:59:47.279
lack of confidence today.

00:59:47.279 --> 00:59:54.619
I think, you know, we could look back today
and say, how on earth did we think a firm

00:59:54.619 --> 00:59:59.099
can navigate 30- or 40- or 50-1 leverage?

00:59:59.099 --> 01:00:05.239
And we have regulators saying how on earth
have we -- did we not, you know, protect society

01:00:05.239 --> 01:00:07.180
better?

01:00:07.180 --> 01:00:12.670
And so we are -- we have moved the pendulum
way too far the other way.

01:00:12.670 --> 01:00:20.250
And I think now in an era of low-interest
rates to navigate, you know, our way out of

01:00:20.250 --> 01:00:29.259
this problem, hopefully through low-interest
rates, we do stabilize the situation, but

01:00:29.259 --> 01:00:33.970
most importantly I think we haven't changed
our behavior.

01:00:33.970 --> 01:00:38.680
Because of the fears of the precipice of the
result of '08 and '09, I think people are

01:00:38.680 --> 01:00:47.170
just standing there, mostly (uninvested ?), sitting
in (too ?) large of low-yielding instruments

01:00:47.170 --> 01:00:53.140
and they're not addressing their longer-term
issue and that is, what do I do with my money?

01:00:53.140 --> 01:00:59.540
How do I prepare for my future the same way
I prepare for my health future, (right ?) ? There's

01:00:59.540 --> 01:01:06.119
just too much commentary, maybe not enough
on your health, but there's no commentary

01:01:06.119 --> 01:01:11.059
about how to have a standard of living that
meets your future needs.

01:01:11.059 --> 01:01:16.180
&gt;&gt;BARTIROMO: So barring people just hearing
what you're saying and saying, OK, look, I

01:01:16.180 --> 01:01:20.170
need to make a change in my own financial
life, do you see -- as we wrap up here, do

01:01:20.170 --> 01:01:26.280
you see a catalyst on the horizon that will
trigger growth, reignite growth, reignite

01:01:26.280 --> 01:01:30.410
that money moving into -- and to make it work
for us?

01:01:30.410 --> 01:01:34.859
For example, will the election in November
be a catalyst for the economy?

01:01:34.859 --> 01:01:38.329
&gt;&gt;FINK: That's a long ways away.

01:01:38.329 --> 01:01:43.930
In -- I think we're starting -- you know,
I think if we continue to see stability in

01:01:43.930 --> 01:01:50.209
financial markets, I think on the margin,
you're going to see a behavior change.

01:01:50.209 --> 01:01:53.539
And through that change of behavior, you're
going to -- you will see some form of job

01:01:53.539 --> 01:01:54.539
growth.

01:01:54.539 --> 01:01:55.589
You're starting to see that now.

01:01:55.589 --> 01:02:01.480
You're starting to see, on the margin, an
improvement in jobs.

01:02:01.480 --> 01:02:08.279
We have to remember 2011 we experienced 30,000
fewer jobs per month on average because state

01:02:08.279 --> 01:02:11.189
and local governments cutting back.

01:02:11.189 --> 01:02:21.039
And so much of the problems last year of anemic
job growth was the public sector -- of paring

01:02:21.039 --> 01:02:22.039
down their payrolls.

01:02:22.039 --> 01:02:23.609
&gt;&gt;BARTIROMO: Is that still going on?

01:02:23.609 --> 01:02:27.199
&gt;&gt;FINK: Well, we have -- we will still have
those pressures.

01:02:27.199 --> 01:02:32.699
That is why I said in my speech we need to
have this partnership between government and

01:02:32.699 --> 01:02:34.140
the private sector.

01:02:34.140 --> 01:02:38.859
If we are going to have above-trendline growth,
it's not going to come from the public sector.

01:02:38.859 --> 01:02:40.769
It's going to come from the private sector.

01:02:40.769 --> 01:02:48.940
You know, we've lived in a -- in a nation
in which the public sector has grown in jobs.

01:02:48.940 --> 01:02:52.680
The public sector, as we saw in 2011, started
shrinking those jobs.

01:02:52.680 --> 01:02:57.380
That will continue, and so we need to find
-- you know, we need to have a more vibrant

01:02:57.380 --> 01:02:59.449
private sector to continue to build jobs.

01:02:59.449 --> 01:03:04.599
You know, I could go on in many other ways
that we -- I think we could do that, whether

01:03:04.599 --> 01:03:08.849
it's -- you know, there's a proposal for tax
regime changes, which I don't wish to get

01:03:08.849 --> 01:03:09.849
into.

01:03:09.849 --> 01:03:11.910
But among those things are ways to stimulate
job growth.

01:03:11.910 --> 01:03:13.869
&gt;&gt;BARTIROMO: And these will be catalysts,
then?

01:03:13.869 --> 01:03:14.869
&gt;&gt;FINK: Yes.

01:03:14.869 --> 01:03:15.869
&gt;&gt;BARTIROMO: Yeah.

01:03:15.869 --> 01:03:19.230
Extraordinary that we're actually seeing,
by the way, leadership change possibly in

01:03:19.230 --> 01:03:24.690
France coming up, in China, potentially in
the United States --

01:03:24.690 --> 01:03:27.180
&gt;&gt;FINK: Germany next -- early next year.

01:03:27.180 --> 01:03:28.180
&gt;&gt;BARTIROMO: Germany as well.

01:03:28.180 --> 01:03:30.009
So really a world leadership change.

01:03:30.009 --> 01:03:33.260
Larry, thank you so much.

01:03:33.260 --> 01:03:37.940
Thank, everyone.

01:03:37.940 --> 01:03:44.960
&gt;&gt;FINK: Thanks, Maria.

01:03:44.960 --> 01:03:49.640
Thank you.

